WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.939
What if doing well in school and in life

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:09.630
depends on much more than your ability to
learn quickly and easily?

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.710
I started studying kids and adults

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.940
in all kinds of super challenging settings,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.980
and in every study my question was,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.259
"Who is successful here and why?"

00:00:20.259 --> 00:00:24.289
My research team and I went to West Point
Military Academy.

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:28.060
We tried to predict which cadets would stay
in military training

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.289
and which would drop out.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:33.120
We went to the National Spelling Bee and tried
to predict

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.160
which children would advance farthest in competition.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.809
We partnered with private companies, asking,

00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:41.460
"Which of these salespeople is going to keep
their jobs?

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.600
And who's going to earn the most money?"

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.140
In all those very different contexts,

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.870
one characteristic emerged as a significant
predictor of success.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.429
And it wasn't social intelligence.

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:59.070
It wasn't good looks, physical health, and
it wasn't I.Q.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.499
It was grit.

00:01:01.499 --> 00:01:06.580
Grit is passion and perseverance for very
long-term goals.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.760
Grit is having stamina.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.110
Grit is sticking with your future,

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.400
day in, day out, not just for the week,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.980
not just for the month, but for years,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.640
and working really hard to make that future
a reality.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:26.680
A few years ago, I started studying grit in
the Chicago public schools.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.140
I asked thousands of high school juniors to
take grit questionnaires,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.180
and then waited around more than a year to
see who would graduate.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.380
Turns out that grittier kids

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.590
were significantly more likely to graduate,

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.220
even when I matched them on every characteristic

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.960
I could measure, things like family income,

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.390
standardized achievement test scores,

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.070
even how safe kids felt when they were at
school.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.480
So it's not just at West Point

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.170
or the National Spelling Bee that grit matters.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:02.220
It's also in school, especially for kids at
risk for dropping out.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.240
Every day, parents and teachers ask me,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.560
"How do I build grit in kids?"

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.550
So far, the best idea I've heard about building
grit in kids

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.380
is something called "growth mindset."

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.590
This is an idea developed

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.340
at Stanford University by Carol Dweck,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.390
and it is the belief that the ability to learn
is not fixed,

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.850
that it can change with your effort.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.280
Dr. Dweck has shown that when kids

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.700
read and learn about the brain

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.530
and how it changes and grows in response to
challenge,

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.470
they're much more likely to persevere when
they fail,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:43.170
because they don't believe that failure is
a permanent condition.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.780
So growth mindset is a great idea for building
grit.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.610
But we need more.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:52.569
We need to take our best ideas, our strongest
intuitions,

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:54.530
and we need to test them.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.380
We need to measure whether we've been successful,

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.959
and we have to be willing to fail, to be wrong,

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:03.359
to start over again with lessons learned.

